00:10:26.080 --> 00:10:27.400
Shine it up.

00:10:27.400 --> 00:10:28.560
Trim it down.

00:10:28.560 --> 00:10:29.610
But,

00:10:29.610 --> 00:10:30.910
Tame, tame those cuticles.

00:10:30.910 --> 00:10:34.970
The unfortunate thing is that
both of my kids were sick

00:10:34.970 --> 00:10:36.320
and had to stay home from school.

00:10:36.320 --> 00:10:37.230
Ooh.

00:10:37.230 --> 00:10:38.787
And so then Jessie was like,

00:10:38.787 --> 00:10:42.120
"Well, I mean I feel like we
should stay here with 'em"

00:10:42.120 --> 00:10:43.390
and I'm like, "I feel like we should

00:10:43.390 --> 00:10:44.719
leave them to themselves."

00:10:47.020 --> 00:10:49.150
Dad got a day off,

00:10:49.150 --> 00:10:50.920
he was thinking he was going to get

00:10:50.920 --> 00:10:53.710
to have a day date with his wife,

00:10:53.710 --> 00:10:54.543
Yeah.

00:10:54.543 --> 00:10:56.080
and so I still wanted that to happen.

00:10:56.080 --> 00:10:56.913
Sure.

00:10:56.913 --> 00:10:58.320
And she was like, "Well,
what are they gonna eat?"

00:10:58.320 --> 00:11:00.690
I was like, "Well, there's a kitchen."

00:11:00.690 --> 00:11:03.120
You know, there's a kitchen
there, there's food in there.

00:11:03.120 --> 00:11:03.953
How sick were they?

00:11:03.953 --> 00:11:05.773
Were they like bed-ridden or
they were just like sniffles?

00:11:05.773 --> 00:11:10.600
They were like, it was a cold

00:11:10.600 --> 00:11:12.820
and maybe a little bit
of a fever for Shepherd,

00:11:12.820 --> 00:11:15.220
so it was like ah, this
wouldn't be responsible,

00:11:16.155 --> 00:11:17.730
this would be irresponsible
to let them go,

00:11:17.730 --> 00:11:18.630
To school, yeah.

00:11:19.860 --> 00:11:20.760
So did you leave 'em?

00:11:20.760 --> 00:11:22.240
Yeah, we left 'em.
Did you win?

00:11:22.240 --> 00:11:23.720
Yeah and then Jessie,

00:11:23.720 --> 00:11:24.733
And when you came back,
were they all right?

00:11:24.733 --> 00:11:25.700
They were still there.

00:11:25.700 --> 00:11:26.820
Yeah, so, good choice.

00:11:26.820 --> 00:11:28.770
I mean, Jessie would say things like,

00:11:29.770 --> 00:11:32.807
she told 'em, she said,
"Okay, there's some pasta,

00:11:32.807 --> 00:11:34.890
"there's some pasta in the
refrigerator that you can",

00:11:34.890 --> 00:11:38.150
like some ravioli-type thing, you know?

00:11:38.150 --> 00:11:38.983
Okay.

00:11:38.983 --> 00:11:42.560
It's in the fancy Italian section of the,

00:11:42.560 --> 00:11:44.050
I call it the fancy Italian section

00:11:44.050 --> 00:11:48.880
of the frozen, of the cold
part of the grocery store

00:11:48.880 --> 00:11:52.940
where it's like the
pasta that isn't frozen,

00:11:52.940 --> 00:11:53.919
it's in that plastic thing

00:11:53.919 --> 00:11:54.752
Oh yeah.

00:11:54.752 --> 00:11:55.633
And you can throw it
in there and boil it up

00:11:55.633 --> 00:11:58.500
then you feel a little
bit more like a chef.

00:11:58.500 --> 00:12:00.310
All you did was boil some pasta.

00:12:00.310 --> 00:12:02.680
But it was never frozen, so it's fresh.

00:12:02.680 --> 00:12:04.050
Yeah it was never frozen
so it feels fancy.

00:12:04.050 --> 00:12:04.883
Yeah.

00:12:04.883 --> 00:12:07.510
It feels kinda like the
Italian food version

00:12:07.510 --> 00:12:08.820
of a pedicure.

00:12:08.820 --> 00:12:12.630
Yeah, so you're just
throwing that at the kids.

00:12:12.630 --> 00:12:14.530
Well, they can make that.

00:12:14.530 --> 00:12:18.247
Locke can boil that
and then Jessie's like,

00:12:18.247 --> 00:12:21.350
"Well, what if he doesn't
turn the stove off?"

00:12:21.350 --> 00:12:23.027
I'm like, "Well, it'll just be, you know,

00:12:23.027 --> 00:12:25.636
"it'll be a stove that is on."

00:12:25.636 --> 00:12:30.230
Because stove's can be on, right?

00:12:30.230 --> 00:12:32.840
How long a stove is on

00:12:32.840 --> 00:12:36.780
doesn't necessarily
increase the danger, right?

00:12:36.780 --> 00:12:40.690
But the burner could be off
but the gas could still be on.

00:12:40.690 --> 00:12:41.523
Then you gotta problem.

00:12:41.523 --> 00:12:44.130
That's a problem but
that's kinda hard to do.

00:12:44.130 --> 00:12:45.300
Yeah.

00:12:45.300 --> 00:12:47.417
But I did text Locke and said,

00:12:47.417 --> 00:12:51.537
"Hey man, if you turn the stove on,

00:12:51.537 --> 00:12:52.777
"make sure you turn it off,"

00:12:52.777 --> 00:12:54.840
and he was like, "Okay."

00:12:54.840 --> 00:12:56.950
And when you got home, what was burned?

00:12:56.950 --> 00:12:57.783
Nothing.

00:12:57.783 --> 00:12:58.616
Nothing was burned.

00:12:58.616 --> 00:12:59.449
That's good.

00:12:59.449 --> 00:13:02.117
They had full bellies and,

00:13:02.117 --> 00:13:04.280
But what did you do?

00:13:04.280 --> 00:13:06.740
I went to a restaurant.

00:13:06.740 --> 00:13:07.573
Okay.

00:13:07.573 --> 00:13:09.180
Had a brunch-ish thing.

00:13:09.180 --> 00:13:10.090
Had a brunch.

00:13:10.090 --> 00:13:11.750
And then we went grocery shopping.

00:13:11.750 --> 00:13:12.583
Oh yeah.

00:13:12.583 --> 00:13:13.530
Now we're to it.

00:13:13.530 --> 00:13:14.363
Yeah.

00:13:14.363 --> 00:13:15.440
Romance.

00:13:15.440 --> 00:13:17.860
I mean, I could spend a
lotta time in Whole Foods.

00:13:17.860 --> 00:13:18.693
Really?

00:13:18.693 --> 00:13:19.526
Yeah.

00:13:19.526 --> 00:13:23.590
Yeah, Whole Foods is kinda like,

00:13:23.590 --> 00:13:26.230
there is a sense of
discovery when you go there.

00:13:26.230 --> 00:13:27.063
Yeah.

00:13:27.063 --> 00:13:28.427
Unless you go there a lot.

00:13:28.427 --> 00:13:29.260
I don't.

00:13:29.260 --> 00:13:30.093
I don't, I don't go there a lot.

00:13:30.093 --> 00:13:31.457
Yeah, so it's kinda like, whoa,

00:13:31.457 --> 00:13:34.640
they got this fancy, they
gotta lot of fancy crap.

00:13:34.640 --> 00:13:35.473
Well,

00:13:35.473 --> 00:13:36.306
And then it's like,

00:13:36.306 --> 00:13:38.330
I wanted to come home and I
wanted to grill something.

00:13:38.330 --> 00:13:39.163
Okay.

00:13:39.163 --> 00:13:44.163
And they just have, the meat section

00:13:44.180 --> 00:13:45.773
is just so impressive.

00:13:46.610 --> 00:13:47.920
The meat section?

00:13:47.920 --> 00:13:49.060
Have you seen the meat section

00:13:49.060 --> 00:13:49.920
No.
in Whole Foods?

00:13:49.920 --> 00:13:53.083
I mean, it puts other
meat sections to shame.

00:13:54.120 --> 00:13:57.440
I'm more of a like the weird beverage.

00:13:57.440 --> 00:13:58.700
They got weird beverages, yeah.

00:13:58.700 --> 00:14:00.520
And peanut butter spread sections.

00:14:00.520 --> 00:14:01.990
They got that too.

00:14:01.990 --> 00:14:03.670
What did you get?

00:14:03.670 --> 00:14:05.150
Chicken thighs.

00:14:05.150 --> 00:14:06.940
That doesn't seem odd.

00:14:06.940 --> 00:14:07.773
No.

00:14:07.773 --> 00:14:08.606
Doesn't seem very,

00:14:08.606 --> 00:14:10.120
There was a lot of things
that I could've gotten

00:14:10.120 --> 00:14:11.460
that I couldn't have gotten anywhere else.

00:14:11.460 --> 00:14:16.170
Like they had a giant, just
a giant tomahawk steak.

00:14:16.170 --> 00:14:17.003
Oh yeah.

00:14:17.003 --> 00:14:18.130
Just I mean,

00:14:18.130 --> 00:14:19.077
That's kind of intimidating.

00:14:19.077 --> 00:14:19.910
Just obscene.

00:14:19.910 --> 00:14:21.970
Like I don't even know if
it would fit on my grill.

00:14:21.970 --> 00:14:23.790
And don't wanna mess that up.

00:14:23.790 --> 00:14:24.810
And um,

00:14:24.810 --> 00:14:26.077
You can't go wrong with chicken thighs.

00:14:26.077 --> 00:14:28.670
But I do wanna get one
of those at one point.

00:14:28.670 --> 00:14:33.200
Daddy cooks a tomahawk
and then I just come in,

00:14:33.200 --> 00:14:37.020
holding it by the bone and
let the family eat off of it

00:14:37.020 --> 00:14:39.840
like a giant meat drumstick.

00:14:39.840 --> 00:14:41.890
So then, you could rent like a cave.

00:14:41.890 --> 00:14:43.760
You could like all go into a cave,

00:14:43.760 --> 00:14:44.600
No, I wanna do it in the kitchen.

00:14:44.600 --> 00:14:46.220
You could wear like skins.

00:14:46.220 --> 00:14:49.080
You could show up with a big hunk of meat.

00:14:49.080 --> 00:14:51.230
And but no, I got chicken thighs.

00:14:51.230 --> 00:14:53.490
Man, chicken thighs.

00:14:53.490 --> 00:14:55.960
Can't beat a good chicken
thigh on the grill, man.

00:14:55.960 --> 00:14:57.260
I put some rub on 'em.

00:14:57.260 --> 00:14:58.093
Boneless?

00:14:59.090 --> 00:15:00.890
Skinless?
No, I went bone in, skin on.

00:15:00.890 --> 00:15:02.180
Oh yeah.

00:15:02.180 --> 00:15:05.140
Even though I talked
about getting boneless

00:15:05.140 --> 00:15:07.247
but Jessie was like, "No, no,
you gotta get the bone in"

00:15:07.247 --> 00:15:09.350
and I understand why.

00:15:09.350 --> 00:15:13.080
It's just a little, it's
a little nastier to eat

00:15:13.080 --> 00:15:15.450
'cause of the dark meat and you get down

00:15:15.450 --> 00:15:17.610
right next to the bone and no
matter how well you cooked it,

00:15:17.610 --> 00:15:19.130
it's a little pink right next to the bone

00:15:19.130 --> 00:15:21.100
and you're kinda like,
"Did I cook this enough?"

00:15:21.100 --> 00:15:21.933
Oh.

00:15:21.933 --> 00:15:23.770
And you're asking yourself that question.

00:15:25.260 --> 00:15:27.117
But no, I put some rub on 'em

00:15:27.117 --> 00:15:32.117
and then I cooked 'em on
some hot coals, charcoal,

00:15:32.420 --> 00:15:34.850
then I put some barbecue
sauce on at the end

00:15:34.850 --> 00:15:36.860
and let it caramelize a little bit,

00:15:36.860 --> 00:15:38.600
you know, blacken 'em up a little bit.

00:15:38.600 --> 00:15:39.700
Um hm.

00:15:39.700 --> 00:15:43.270
Also grilled some poblano peppers,

00:15:43.270 --> 00:15:44.103
Oh!

00:15:44.103 --> 00:15:44.936
along with them.

00:15:44.936 --> 00:15:45.769
The California ones.

00:15:45.769 --> 00:15:47.380
Fire-roasted poblanos.

00:15:47.380 --> 00:15:48.910
Cut those up, put in
the middle of the table.

00:15:48.910 --> 00:15:51.060
You could have 'em
along with your chicken.

00:15:52.670 --> 00:15:55.400
Everybody was happy with dad,
at least for a little bit.

00:15:55.400 --> 00:15:57.190
Okay, that's good.

00:15:57.190 --> 00:15:58.360
But your toes though.

00:15:58.360 --> 00:16:00.500
I mean, my toes were in shoes
the whole time, nobody saw,

00:16:00.500 --> 00:16:01.991
I think your toes need work.

00:16:01.991 --> 00:16:03.820
I've seen 'em occasionally, those nails,

00:16:03.820 --> 00:16:06.750
I mean I think you
really stand to benefit.

00:16:06.750 --> 00:16:08.610
It's not a,

00:16:08.610 --> 00:16:09.530
It needs to be done.

00:16:09.530 --> 00:16:12.019
No, it's not a lack of care.

00:16:12.019 --> 00:16:12.852
I--

00:16:12.852 --> 00:16:14.810
It has nothing to do
with care, it's genetics.

00:16:14.810 --> 00:16:15.755
Yeah.

00:16:15.755 --> 00:16:16.588
I could get,

00:16:16.588 --> 00:16:19.178
They can buff it and shape
it, they can do something.

00:16:19.178 --> 00:16:20.128
I don't know why.

00:16:21.010 --> 00:16:22.100
For who?

00:16:22.100 --> 00:16:23.910
Who would it be for?

00:16:23.910 --> 00:16:24.743
You?

00:16:26.000 --> 00:16:27.300
'Cause it's not for me.

00:16:27.300 --> 00:16:28.690
I don't care.

00:16:28.690 --> 00:16:30.260
I feel like the way your nails grow,

00:16:30.260 --> 00:16:31.870
they like grow up and out.

00:16:31.870 --> 00:16:35.447
No, they grow, they
hug the end of the toe.

00:16:36.849 --> 00:16:39.700
They don't grow out, they
just go around the toes.

00:16:39.700 --> 00:16:40.533
That's good.

00:16:40.533 --> 00:16:41.366
Maybe you never need to cut 'em then.

00:16:41.366 --> 00:16:42.199
Like a claw.

00:16:43.590 --> 00:16:45.370
It's like your toes have a helmet

00:16:45.370 --> 00:16:47.210
if you let it keep growing.

00:16:47.210 --> 00:16:48.043
A little nail helmet.

00:16:48.043 --> 00:16:50.240
I keep 'em trimmed, for the most part.

00:16:50.240 --> 00:16:51.073
It's just,

00:16:51.929 --> 00:16:53.170
That's a real long ways for you.

00:16:53.170 --> 00:16:54.450
They're just unsightly.

00:16:54.450 --> 00:16:56.700
To get down there to that toe.

00:16:56.700 --> 00:16:58.800
I don't, to be honest with you,

00:16:58.800 --> 00:17:02.290
and this is probably sexist.

00:17:02.290 --> 00:17:06.073
I just don't trust a man
with good-lookin' feet.

00:17:07.040 --> 00:17:08.220
Hm.

00:17:08.220 --> 00:17:10.813
You know, I just, I feel
like he's up to something.

00:17:12.120 --> 00:17:14.400
That explains why whenever
we have an important meeting

00:17:14.400 --> 00:17:16.660
or interview you ask people

00:17:16.660 --> 00:17:18.920
to take off their shoes and socks.

00:17:18.920 --> 00:17:19.753
Right.

00:17:19.753 --> 00:17:21.730
And if it's a man and he
has like spectacular feet,

00:17:21.730 --> 00:17:25.479
I'm like, "You know, I don't know.

00:17:25.479 --> 00:17:27.030
"I don't know if I can trust you."

00:17:27.030 --> 00:17:28.680
Yeah, that's probably sexist.

00:17:28.680 --> 00:17:31.250
It's probably multiple things.

00:17:31.250 --> 00:17:32.390
I'm just being honest.

00:17:32.390 --> 00:17:34.230
Speaking of being
honest, I wanna be honest

00:17:34.230 --> 00:17:37.260
about what it's like to
do the self-promotion

00:17:37.260 --> 00:17:38.980
so we're gonna get into that.

00:17:38.980 --> 00:17:41.923
Well, I think on the calendar
we called it media day.

00:17:44.079 --> 00:17:45.750
The day in particular,
we did a number of things

00:17:45.750 --> 00:17:48.330
but the day that I am
thinking about in particular

00:17:48.330 --> 00:17:53.030
is Monday of this past week
when were recording this,

00:17:53.030 --> 00:17:54.420
in which we had to

00:17:54.420 --> 00:17:55.460
In New York City.

00:17:55.460 --> 00:17:56.660
We had to get up.

00:17:56.660 --> 00:17:57.810
I'm just gonna give like
an overview of the day

00:17:57.810 --> 00:17:59.540
and then we can get into the details.

00:17:59.540 --> 00:18:00.740
Okay.

00:18:00.740 --> 00:18:05.740
We had to get up, get dressed,
go to The Today Show first?

00:18:08.700 --> 00:18:09.970
I think so, yeah.

00:18:09.970 --> 00:18:14.800
Then we had to go to Conde Nast,

00:18:14.800 --> 00:18:19.800
who owns Wired and Vanity Fair,

00:18:19.810 --> 00:18:23.053
do three videos with them,

00:18:24.230 --> 00:18:25.750
and then immediately go

00:18:25.750 --> 00:18:28.723
to The Tonight Show with Jimmy Fallon.

00:18:30.550 --> 00:18:34.030
And then go directly from The
Tonight Show with Jimmy Fallon

00:18:34.030 --> 00:18:36.780
to The Bleak Creek Conversations

00:18:36.780 --> 00:18:37.920
and that's why we showed up

00:18:37.920 --> 00:18:40.593
at that particular show in our suits.

00:18:41.760 --> 00:18:44.050
When I say it like that,
it doesn't feel like

00:18:44.050 --> 00:18:46.560
it was a whole lot but it certainly felt

00:18:46.560 --> 00:18:48.350
like a whole lot at the time.

00:18:48.350 --> 00:18:49.870
Well, especially with the time change.

00:18:49.870 --> 00:18:51.220
I mean, it's three hours difference

00:18:51.220 --> 00:18:53.463
so you're having to get up,

00:18:54.860 --> 00:18:56.410
let's just say three hours earlier

00:18:56.410 --> 00:18:57.953
than we normally would.

00:18:57.953 --> 00:18:59.710
I definitely had not yet adjusted yet.

00:18:59.710 --> 00:19:04.100
So that's kinda, and it
was the second or third day

00:19:04.100 --> 00:19:06.450
of having been on the time, you know,

00:19:06.450 --> 00:19:08.940
that's when the jet lag really hits.

00:19:08.940 --> 00:19:11.080
So it was pretty difficult

00:19:11.080 --> 00:19:14.180
but I mean, you go on the Today Show.

00:19:14.180 --> 00:19:17.060
With all these, with the television shows,

00:19:17.060 --> 00:19:19.533
it's true of Fallon, it's
true of the Today Show,

00:19:20.650 --> 00:19:22.390
and any other appearance like that,

00:19:22.390 --> 00:19:25.930
you do this pre-interview
where you're kinda talking

00:19:25.930 --> 00:19:28.460
to the producer in order to, you know,

00:19:28.460 --> 00:19:29.870
they know what you wanna promote.

00:19:29.870 --> 00:19:31.560
It's like we're talking
to them about the novel

00:19:31.560 --> 00:19:33.000
so that they can find a touch point

00:19:33.000 --> 00:19:35.380
that works with their audience
so they can ask us a question

00:19:35.380 --> 00:19:39.380
that then we can say
something that'll resonate.

00:19:39.380 --> 00:19:41.080
But then you also, you gotta try

00:19:41.080 --> 00:19:45.400
to get a funny story in
there or like play a game.

00:19:45.400 --> 00:19:48.860
Apparently, you know, they're
throwing more and more things

00:19:48.860 --> 00:19:52.850
at guest appearances in order
to get people to watch them.

00:19:52.850 --> 00:19:54.940
We can unpack that more
but I think when we went

00:19:54.940 --> 00:19:56.630
to the Today Show and The Tonight Show,

00:19:58.180 --> 00:20:01.720
it's like we kinda knew based on

00:20:01.720 --> 00:20:05.130
the pre-interview with the producers

00:20:05.130 --> 00:20:06.980
what it is we were gonna be doing.

00:20:06.980 --> 00:20:10.023
But I will say that that interview went,

00:20:11.260 --> 00:20:14.300
it was very short, but it went a lot,

00:20:14.300 --> 00:20:16.990
it actually wasn't what we had been asked

00:20:16.990 --> 00:20:19.840
in the pre-interview, unlike Fallon,

00:20:19.840 --> 00:20:21.540
which we can get into in a second.

00:20:24.533 --> 00:20:26.420
Well, let's back up a little bit

00:20:26.420 --> 00:20:28.880
and talk about thinking
about what we were gonna wear

00:20:28.880 --> 00:20:29.713
because
Okay .

00:20:29.713 --> 00:20:32.190
that was the first thing that happened.

00:20:32.190 --> 00:20:37.000
All right, we have this wonderful stylist,

00:20:38.150 --> 00:20:43.100
Kimmy Erin, who is the person
who helps us figure out

00:20:43.100 --> 00:20:45.400
what we're gonna wear when
make these media appearances.

00:20:45.400 --> 00:20:47.530
If you think that we looked
good on Jimmy Fallon,

00:20:47.530 --> 00:20:48.694
you can thank Kimmy Erin.

00:20:48.694 --> 00:20:49.930
Um hm.

00:20:49.930 --> 00:20:52.210
And she also put together these looks

00:20:52.210 --> 00:20:54.280
for the Today Show and then

00:20:56.750 --> 00:20:57.583
We had another day

00:20:57.583 --> 00:20:59.730
of stuff we needed to do.
A Chicago thing, yeah.

00:20:59.730 --> 00:21:04.730
So I think that we have
this, we've discussed this,

00:21:07.130 --> 00:21:10.590
we're comfortable in two
different modes typically,

00:21:10.590 --> 00:21:14.760
when it comes to what we're
wearing, and that is casual

00:21:14.760 --> 00:21:16.400
Whatever we would wear
on Good Mythical Morning.

00:21:16.400 --> 00:21:18.010
Which is whatever we would wear on GMM.

00:21:18.010 --> 00:21:21.030
Which is basically what
we would wear, you know,

00:21:21.030 --> 00:21:23.520
if I was just getting up
and going about my day.

00:21:23.520 --> 00:21:24.549
Right.

00:21:24.549 --> 00:21:28.600
And then we have very
dressed up, sharply dressed

00:21:28.600 --> 00:21:29.810
like we were on Fallon.

00:21:29.810 --> 00:21:34.020
Full suit, tie, socks that match, etc.

00:21:34.020 --> 00:21:36.820
I like to wear, it's fun to get dressed up

00:21:36.820 --> 00:21:39.240
every now and again,
especially when it's something

00:21:39.240 --> 00:21:42.700
that's important to us that you can

00:21:42.700 --> 00:21:44.210
be justified getting that fancy.

00:21:44.210 --> 00:21:45.570
Like being on the Tonight Show
And a lot of people

00:21:45.570 --> 00:21:48.630
A lot of people take a different tack

00:21:48.630 --> 00:21:50.800
when they go on the Tonight Show.

00:21:50.800 --> 00:21:54.150
Saw Tyler Ninja.

00:21:54.150 --> 00:21:55.800
He went on the Tonight Show in a hoodie.

00:21:55.800 --> 00:21:57.490
He was dressed like I am right now.

00:21:57.490 --> 00:21:59.550
Like a boxer getting ready to weigh in.

00:21:59.550 --> 00:22:01.480
Let's say a gamer.

00:22:01.480 --> 00:22:02.313
Yeah.

00:22:02.313 --> 00:22:03.146
He was dressed like a gamer.

00:22:03.146 --> 00:22:03.979
And so he was like,

00:22:05.080 --> 00:22:06.960
and everybody does a different thing.

00:22:06.960 --> 00:22:09.030
We're like, "Nah," I said,
"we're gonna dress up."

00:22:09.030 --> 00:22:12.700
But there's this what you
might call a no man's land

00:22:12.700 --> 00:22:16.760
for us which is this, would
you call it business casual?

00:22:16.760 --> 00:22:19.690
It's called the way we
looked on the Today Show.

00:22:19.690 --> 00:22:24.100
So I had this like black
jacket over a turtleneck

00:22:24.100 --> 00:22:27.773
and these tan pants and these black shoes.

00:22:28.770 --> 00:22:31.860
I would very rarely if ever choose

00:22:31.860 --> 00:22:34.880
to wear any of these
things for myself, right?

00:22:34.880 --> 00:22:37.520
I mean, I thought you looked good in it.

00:22:37.520 --> 00:22:39.840
I mean, the pants that
I wore were purple pants

00:22:39.840 --> 00:22:44.700
from a suit that I had but
then I had this sweater,

00:22:44.700 --> 00:22:49.700
this like thin, very dressy,
block pattern sweater

00:22:49.820 --> 00:22:50.653
that I wore with it.

00:22:50.653 --> 00:22:51.960
I thought yours was a little less.

00:22:51.960 --> 00:22:56.830
I actually would, I would
wear that like on a nice date.

00:22:56.830 --> 00:22:58.240
But not really, not in L.A.

00:22:58.240 --> 00:22:59.650
There's very few places where

00:22:59.650 --> 00:23:00.483
Yeah.

00:23:00.483 --> 00:23:01.900
you'd go on a nice date.

00:23:01.900 --> 00:23:03.170
Like you don't go out,

00:23:03.170 --> 00:23:05.960
at least I don't like go
out for a date in L.A.,

00:23:05.960 --> 00:23:09.030
even at a really nice restaurant,
and like wear a blazer.

00:23:09.030 --> 00:23:09.953
Yeah.

00:23:09.953 --> 00:23:12.420
I don't just like put
a blazer on over stuff.

00:23:12.420 --> 00:23:15.450
That weird in between stage,

00:23:15.450 --> 00:23:17.980
I just can't find a way to be comfortable.

00:23:17.980 --> 00:23:20.230
But for the Today Show,
we were given that.

00:23:22.500 --> 00:23:24.360
We were given something
in this middle zone,

00:23:24.360 --> 00:23:25.277
the things that we ended up wearing.

00:23:25.277 --> 00:23:26.110
And we had a choice.

00:23:26.110 --> 00:23:26.943
I will say that we have a choice.

00:23:26.943 --> 00:23:29.520
So Kimmy comes in, she has a spread

00:23:29.520 --> 00:23:32.940
of different clothes,
and so she's going for

00:23:32.940 --> 00:23:37.020
this sort of updated, I
would say elevated casual,

00:23:37.020 --> 00:23:40.550
little bit dressed up but
little bit dressed down look

00:23:40.550 --> 00:23:42.640
for the Today Show, because if you look

00:23:42.640 --> 00:23:44.800
at what people wear on the Today Show,

00:23:44.800 --> 00:23:45.830
that's what they wear.

00:23:45.830 --> 00:23:48.680
And so and also you're thinking about,

00:23:48.680 --> 00:23:50.090
it's funny because we have conversations

00:23:50.090 --> 00:23:52.747
with our publisher who says things like,

00:23:52.747 --> 00:23:55.617
"You know, mostly moms
are gonna be watching

00:23:55.617 --> 00:23:58.317
"the Today Show at the hour
that you're going to be on,

00:23:58.317 --> 00:24:00.213
"the fourth hour of the Today Show,

00:24:01.287 --> 00:24:05.890
"and so we want you to impress moms."

00:24:05.890 --> 00:24:06.723
Right.

00:24:06.723 --> 00:24:08.267
"And say something about the book

00:24:08.267 --> 00:24:10.147
"that would make them want to buy the book

00:24:10.147 --> 00:24:11.660
"for their teenager."

00:24:11.660 --> 00:24:13.610
So then we picked out these outfits

00:24:13.610 --> 00:24:15.750
that we just described to you

00:24:16.740 --> 00:24:19.990
and then we packed 'em
and then we're like,

00:24:19.990 --> 00:24:22.257
we're talking on the
flight and we're like,

00:24:22.257 --> 00:24:26.260
"Man, that's", we're like
fretting about these outfits.

00:24:26.260 --> 00:24:27.093
We're like, "Man, I just don't,

00:24:27.093 --> 00:24:28.560
"I just don't feel comfortable".

00:24:28.560 --> 00:24:30.197
And I'm saying things like,

00:24:30.197 --> 00:24:31.407
"You know, you're jacket's so big.

00:24:31.407 --> 00:24:33.477
"You're already so big.

00:24:33.477 --> 00:24:34.487
"You make me look small.

00:24:34.487 --> 00:24:37.237
"Why you gotta wear this big-ass jacket

00:24:37.237 --> 00:24:39.120
"that makes me look even smaller?"

00:24:39.120 --> 00:24:41.077
And so like that's my
qualm and you're like,

00:24:41.077 --> 00:24:44.070
"Well, I'm not even comfortable
wearing this anyway."

00:24:44.070 --> 00:24:45.300
We don't have to talk about that

00:24:45.300 --> 00:24:47.590
but I think that's in your mind.

00:24:47.590 --> 00:24:52.590
It is, yeah, it was my
overthinking of the ensemble.

00:24:55.130 --> 00:24:56.767
But you're like, "I don't
even want to wear this anyway.

00:24:56.767 --> 00:24:59.094
"I don't feel like, it's not us,

00:24:59.094 --> 00:25:00.760
"I don't feel it's comfortable."

00:25:00.760 --> 00:25:01.750
It's like all the stuff
that you just said.

00:25:01.750 --> 00:25:02.840
And so then we tell Jenna.

00:25:02.840 --> 00:25:06.837
We're like, "So, we're
thinking about not wearing

00:25:06.837 --> 00:25:08.417
"the clothes that Kimmy picked out.

00:25:08.417 --> 00:25:09.250
"We're thinking about wearing

00:25:09.250 --> 00:25:12.470
the slightly less dressy
clothes that she picked out

00:25:12.470 --> 00:25:15.120
for the Chicago thing on the Today Show.

00:25:15.120 --> 00:25:16.010
Yeah.

00:25:16.010 --> 00:25:19.000
So she's like, "Okay,
I'll get those ready"

00:25:19.000 --> 00:25:23.920
and then we get a text from Stevie

00:25:25.100 --> 00:25:30.100
and Stevie's like, "So I
heard you guys are thinking

00:25:30.227 --> 00:25:33.067
"about not wearing the
clothes that Kimmy picked out

00:25:33.067 --> 00:25:34.598
"for you for the Today Show."

00:25:36.110 --> 00:25:38.513
That was like uh-oh, here we go.

00:25:41.930 --> 00:25:43.163
'Cause I could tell.

00:25:44.130 --> 00:25:47.630
Now this is, obviously we have
an incredible relationship

00:25:47.630 --> 00:25:49.720
with Stevie and we've
been working with her for,

00:25:49.720 --> 00:25:50.750
how many years has it been?

00:25:50.750 --> 00:25:52.340
Seven or eight years.

00:25:52.340 --> 00:25:55.750
And this is often the
nature of our relationship.

00:25:55.750 --> 00:25:59.650
This is what we want her to do.

00:25:59.650 --> 00:26:00.840
In fact, when you wished her

00:26:00.840 --> 00:26:03.030
a happy birthday the other
day, you said thank you

00:26:03.030 --> 00:26:04.700
for supporting us,
Disagreeing.

00:26:04.700 --> 00:26:07.500
disagreeing with us and blankety-blank.

00:26:07.500 --> 00:26:10.720
Because she's very good at what she does

00:26:10.720 --> 00:26:12.380
and she's usually right.

00:26:14.030 --> 00:26:17.447
And so, she was like, "Well,
I do think that you do want

00:26:17.447 --> 00:26:18.507
"this to be an elevated look,

00:26:18.507 --> 00:26:20.590
"you are trying to impress
moms and I think that,"

00:26:20.590 --> 00:26:22.750
and she likes sends us the pictures,

00:26:22.750 --> 00:26:24.950
because once you get
through in the wardrobe

00:26:24.950 --> 00:26:26.550
you take a picture of yourself,

00:26:26.550 --> 00:26:28.597
she's like, "This is what
you're thinking about wearing

00:26:28.597 --> 00:26:31.207
"but this is what you could be wearing.

00:26:31.207 --> 00:26:34.107
"I think you should go with
what you could be wearing."

00:26:35.030 --> 00:26:37.410
And I'm like, she's probably right

00:26:37.410 --> 00:26:38.680
but I just want to be comfortable

00:26:38.680 --> 00:26:40.010
and does it really matter anyway?

00:26:40.010 --> 00:26:41.350
Who's gonna watch this?

00:26:41.350 --> 00:26:42.560
I started thinking things like this.

00:26:42.560 --> 00:26:45.200
I'm like, is going on
this show and talking

00:26:45.200 --> 00:26:46.850
about this book for 45 seconds

00:26:46.850 --> 00:26:48.900
gonna make anybody buy it anyway?

00:26:48.900 --> 00:26:50.580
That's the kinda thing I started thinking.

00:26:50.580 --> 00:26:51.840
It's easy to start thinking that,

00:26:51.840 --> 00:26:53.342
especially when you start doing it.

00:26:54.920 --> 00:26:56.327
But yeah, I'm like, listen, okay,

00:26:56.327 --> 00:26:58.760
we 're not gonna die on this hill.

00:26:58.760 --> 00:27:01.040
Like Jenna brought these clothes.

00:27:01.040 --> 00:27:04.370
But then Jenna, so we had
that conversation with Stevie

00:27:04.370 --> 00:27:06.780
and I was like, "Okay, we'll
keep talking about it."

00:27:06.780 --> 00:27:08.390
Neither of us made a definitive decision.

00:27:08.390 --> 00:27:10.710
You got off of the text thread.

00:27:10.710 --> 00:27:12.260
I think you went to work out or something.

00:27:12.260 --> 00:27:13.350
You were like, "I'm not
gonna talk about this now".

00:27:13.350 --> 00:27:14.420
Yeah.

00:27:14.420 --> 00:27:16.600
So after some back and forth with Stevie,

00:27:16.600 --> 00:27:17.700
then I text Jenna.

00:27:17.700 --> 00:27:20.420
I'm like, "Okay, well we
may wear the other clothes."

00:27:20.420 --> 00:27:21.877
And she was like, "I
thought you might say that

00:27:21.877 --> 00:27:23.022
"and I've already got them ready."

00:27:24.463 --> 00:27:25.296
And then

00:27:25.296 --> 00:27:26.129
Yeah, she knows what's up.

00:27:26.129 --> 00:27:29.327
And then she was like, 'I agree that

00:27:29.327 --> 00:27:30.410
"that's what you should wear."

00:27:30.410 --> 00:27:35.410
So okay, Jenna and Stevie now both think

00:27:35.550 --> 00:27:37.990
that this is what we should be wearing

00:27:37.990 --> 00:27:40.920
to impress all the mom's
watching the Today Show

00:27:40.920 --> 00:27:44.449
in the fourth hour and
so that's what we did.

00:27:44.449 --> 00:27:46.110
We wore it.
You know,

00:27:46.110 --> 00:27:47.590
and it's all a game.

00:27:47.590 --> 00:27:52.460
So looking at this as like a case study,

00:27:52.460 --> 00:27:55.390
there's two different
ways you can look at this

00:27:55.390 --> 00:27:58.520
as a listener, I'm thinking.

00:27:58.520 --> 00:28:00.750
One is yeah, this is a game

00:28:00.750 --> 00:28:03.700
and maybe you can see how it gets,

00:28:03.700 --> 00:28:04.860
it can start to wear on you,

00:28:04.860 --> 00:28:08.070
'cause it's like, no pun intended,

00:28:08.070 --> 00:28:09.170
okay, I'm wearing something

00:28:09.170 --> 00:28:11.820
that I wouldn't naturally
wear, even on a date,

00:28:11.820 --> 00:28:14.300
but I'm doing it so that I can reach

00:28:14.300 --> 00:28:16.310
this perceived mom demographic.

00:28:16.310 --> 00:28:18.420
It's like eh.

00:28:18.420 --> 00:28:20.467
But then, on the other hand it's like,

00:28:20.467 --> 00:28:22.730
you've got somebody who's bringing in

00:28:22.730 --> 00:28:25.370
like a spread of clothes
and we just go in, you know,

00:28:25.370 --> 00:28:26.580
we're trying all this stuff on

00:28:26.580 --> 00:28:29.940
and somebody makes us look elevated.

00:28:29.940 --> 00:28:31.853
Isn't that fun, you know?

00:28:33.033 --> 00:28:35.470
And I mean it's definitely
fun to, and it is.

00:28:35.470 --> 00:28:38.270
I think both are true, ya know?

00:28:38.270 --> 00:28:39.810
Especially when you talk about the suits

00:28:39.810 --> 00:28:42.590
and like being able to
like look your finest

00:28:42.590 --> 00:28:46.100
and go on the Tonight
Show for not the first

00:28:46.100 --> 00:28:48.090
but I think the sixth time?

00:28:48.090 --> 00:28:48.923
Yeah.

00:28:48.923 --> 00:28:50.350
Ya know, that's frickin' awesome.

00:28:50.350 --> 00:28:51.290
So it's like.

00:28:51.290 --> 00:28:52.230
No, it's great.

00:28:52.230 --> 00:28:54.330
We're not complaining.

00:28:54.330 --> 00:28:58.943
It's just we think it's amusing,

00:28:59.890 --> 00:29:01.240
the games that you have to play

00:29:01.240 --> 00:29:02.350
when it comes to marketing, you know?

00:29:02.350 --> 00:29:05.270
Well, I will say that in the moment,

00:29:05.270 --> 00:29:08.260
in the moment it is
difficult for me to enjoy.

00:29:08.260 --> 00:29:09.093
Yeah.

00:29:10.260 --> 00:29:14.600
I am often, when that day,

00:29:14.600 --> 00:29:16.403
so I knew that day was coming, right?

00:29:16.403 --> 00:29:18.260
And I was like, okay, we're
gonna do the Today Show.

00:29:18.260 --> 00:29:20.300
By the way, for those of
you who might be confused,

00:29:20.300 --> 00:29:22.900
if you were following along
during the week that we,

00:29:23.790 --> 00:29:26.800
we'd recorded the Today Show
but then it shot, it aired

00:29:26.800 --> 00:29:29.270
that Thursday or Friday so,

00:29:29.270 --> 00:29:31.300
but all this was done on the same day.

00:29:31.300 --> 00:29:32.840
I was thinking about that day

00:29:34.080 --> 00:29:38.100
and we have so little
control in those situations.

00:29:38.100 --> 00:29:40.963
So like for every situation
that we were going into,

00:29:43.687 --> 00:29:44.660
we're not in control.

00:29:44.660 --> 00:29:47.340
We're not hosting, we're
on somebody else's show.

00:29:47.340 --> 00:29:48.173
Um hm.

00:29:49.300 --> 00:29:52.230
And I definitely don't look forward to it.

00:29:52.230 --> 00:29:53.870
I actually start thinking about,

00:29:53.870 --> 00:29:57.300
I had these fantasies about
being in my hotel room

00:29:57.300 --> 00:30:00.770
at the end of the night, going to bed.

00:30:00.770 --> 00:30:02.111
Yep.

00:30:02.111 --> 00:30:05.170
That is not necessarily a
healthy way to see that day

00:30:05.170 --> 00:30:06.150
when you wake up in the morning

00:30:06.150 --> 00:30:06.983
and you're thinking about, ah,

00:30:06.983 --> 00:30:08.840
I just wanna be back here going to bed

00:30:08.840 --> 00:30:11.790
because I'm anxious about
the stuff that I have to do.

00:30:11.790 --> 00:30:14.750
And I don't want to over blow it,

00:30:14.750 --> 00:30:17.080
it's not a debilitating,
it's not like a true,

00:30:17.080 --> 00:30:18.773
it's not like a anxiety disorder.

00:30:19.640 --> 00:30:20.910
I'm not like super uncomfortable,

00:30:20.910 --> 00:30:25.653
it's just I have trouble
getting myself to enjoy this.

00:30:27.130 --> 00:30:30.883
I'd rather be doing this
on my own terms, ya know.

00:30:31.810 --> 00:30:33.740
But then, in the moment,

00:30:33.740 --> 00:30:35.150
especially depending on what it was,

00:30:35.150 --> 00:30:37.860
I think when we were on Fallon

00:30:37.860 --> 00:30:41.260
and we were performing a
song that we had written

00:30:41.260 --> 00:30:44.400
when we were 14, I was
legitimately having a good time,

00:30:44.400 --> 00:30:45.233
Yeah.

00:30:45.233 --> 00:30:46.080
at that point.

00:30:46.080 --> 00:30:47.860
Yeah, that was amazing.

00:30:47.860 --> 00:30:48.905
But for the Today Show,

00:30:48.905 --> 00:30:52.720
I mean, the morning shows are like zoos.

00:30:52.720 --> 00:30:55.550
I mean, it's just like
with all cages open.

00:30:55.550 --> 00:30:59.160
It's just everybody's going
everywhere in cramped quarters

00:30:59.160 --> 00:31:01.480
and you're being shoved here to wait

00:31:01.480 --> 00:31:03.710
and then come out here and
then, oh, let's meet the host

00:31:03.710 --> 00:31:05.370
and oh, there putting blindfolds on you

00:31:05.370 --> 00:31:07.520
and you're eating a
sandwich that they made .

00:31:07.520 --> 00:31:09.287
It's like, we knew we were gonna do that

00:31:09.287 --> 00:31:10.360
and that was funny.

00:31:10.360 --> 00:31:12.230
And then we did the interview

00:31:12.230 --> 00:31:14.830
and it was we knew we were gonna get

00:31:14.830 --> 00:31:16.260
a couple of questions about the book.

00:31:16.260 --> 00:31:18.130
We get the first question,

00:31:18.130 --> 00:31:20.603
'cause we wanna talk to
the moms about the novel.

00:31:21.516 --> 00:31:24.320
You know, we got all
dressed up for you, mom.

00:31:24.320 --> 00:31:26.220
You wanna hear about this novel?

00:31:26.220 --> 00:31:30.450
And so they ask you,
"What's the novel about?",

00:31:30.450 --> 00:31:32.830
and you're like sharing
what the novel's about.

00:31:32.830 --> 00:31:35.190
And then I'm like, okay, I
know what my follow up is,

00:31:35.190 --> 00:31:36.640
just to kind of clarify

00:31:38.210 --> 00:31:41.500
what I think's gonna
resonate with the viewer.

00:31:41.500 --> 00:31:43.050
But I didn't even have a chance

00:31:43.050 --> 00:31:46.000
to get that in because
they move so quickly,

00:31:46.000 --> 00:31:48.700
you're giving your answer
about the brief synopsis

00:31:48.700 --> 00:31:50.300
of "The Lost Causes of Bleak Creek"

00:31:50.300 --> 00:31:52.347
and right on the heels of that she says,

00:31:52.347 --> 00:31:54.190
"You guys wanna play a
game where we zoom in

00:31:54.190 --> 00:31:55.670
on food and you guess what it is?"

00:31:55.670 --> 00:32:00.670
I was like, "Whoa, okay, sure!

00:32:01.190 --> 00:32:03.431
So you know, I've heard
Joe Rogan talk about this.

00:32:03.431 --> 00:32:05.460
It's like being in a rock tumbler.

00:32:05.460 --> 00:32:07.743
Joe Rogan has talked about the fact that,

00:32:08.840 --> 00:32:11.297
he's like, "I hate doing morning radio

00:32:11.297 --> 00:32:14.230
"and I don't wanna do
appearances and interviews

00:32:15.187 --> 00:32:19.047
"so I just have this podcast
and I just tell people

00:32:19.047 --> 00:32:20.567
"that I'm gonna be somewhere and they come

00:32:20.567 --> 00:32:23.930
"to my shows and they sell out
because", and you know what?

00:32:23.930 --> 00:32:25.040
He can do that because he has

00:32:25.040 --> 00:32:27.300
the most popular podcast in the world.

00:32:27.300 --> 00:32:28.170
We can't do that.

00:32:28.170 --> 00:32:32.030
If we just told you guys
on GMM and Ear Biscuits

00:32:32.030 --> 00:32:33.020
about what we were doing,

00:32:33.020 --> 00:32:34.630
a lot less people would find out about it,

00:32:34.630 --> 00:32:37.420
so we don't have the privilege
of being able to make that

00:32:37.420 --> 00:32:39.840
but I understand the impulse

00:32:39.840 --> 00:32:43.390
because even the way
podcasting in general.

00:32:43.390 --> 00:32:45.170
I mean obviously we don't
do interviews anymore

00:32:45.170 --> 00:32:50.170
but the way that everything is put

00:32:51.460 --> 00:32:53.170
into these really bite-sized pieces.

00:32:53.170 --> 00:32:58.170
Even when you've got a daily
show like the Today Show,

00:32:58.780 --> 00:33:02.650
which I guess goes for, is
it four hours every day?

00:33:02.650 --> 00:33:05.100
I don't know, it's at
least four hours every day.

00:33:06.184 --> 00:33:07.173
Well, at least three but go ahead.

00:33:07.173 --> 00:33:08.006
'Cause we were in the fourth,

00:33:08.006 --> 00:33:08.980
they say we were in the fourth hour

00:33:08.980 --> 00:33:11.230
so it's gotta be at
least four hours, right?

00:33:11.230 --> 00:33:13.610
Yeah, that's when they don't
worry about the news anymore

00:33:13.610 --> 00:33:15.270
and they start zooming in on pictures of

00:33:15.270 --> 00:33:16.103
Right,

00:33:16.103 --> 00:33:17.290
and so
food.

00:33:17.290 --> 00:33:20.460
if you have got to talk

00:33:20.460 --> 00:33:22.270
and fill that time every single day,

00:33:22.270 --> 00:33:25.130
you would think this might be a place

00:33:25.130 --> 00:33:30.080
for a slightly slower
paced, relaxed conversation

00:33:30.080 --> 00:33:34.550
about something but no, even
in that, four hours of content,

00:33:34.550 --> 00:33:36.200
it's gonna be, "What's your book about?"

00:33:36.200 --> 00:33:37.537
You say one line and you say,

00:33:37.537 --> 00:33:39.750
"All right, let's play a game
where we zoom in on food."

00:33:39.750 --> 00:33:40.930
Um hm.

00:33:40.930 --> 00:33:42.710
That's just the nature of the medium.

00:33:42.710 --> 00:33:45.600
I'm not necessarily complaining about it

00:33:45.600 --> 00:33:46.930
but I'm just saying, you have to know that

00:33:46.930 --> 00:33:48.860
that is the environment you're going into

00:33:48.860 --> 00:33:51.830
as opposed to when we made the appearance

00:33:51.830 --> 00:33:54.080
on the Dead Meat podcast

00:33:56.710 --> 00:33:58.250
and Pete Holmes' podcast.

00:33:58.250 --> 00:33:59.083
Yeah.

00:33:59.083 --> 00:34:00.700
And the Books On The Subway podcast,

00:34:00.700 --> 00:34:01.533
which we did in New York.

00:34:01.533 --> 00:34:02.430
Basically any podcast.

00:34:02.430 --> 00:34:03.263
Any podcast.

00:34:03.263 --> 00:34:04.096
Any podcast you can be like,

00:34:04.096 --> 00:34:06.710
I'm gonna talk about this at my own pace

00:34:06.710 --> 00:34:07.730
and I'm gonna get to say the things

00:34:07.730 --> 00:34:08.750
that I wanna say about it.

00:34:08.750 --> 00:34:13.350
Which by the way, if
you're, I do recommend you

00:34:13.350 --> 00:34:14.750
listening to those podcasts.

00:34:15.640 --> 00:34:17.210
If you're reading the novel right now

00:34:17.210 --> 00:34:19.070
and you're into the details of it

00:34:19.070 --> 00:34:21.420
but you want a spoiler-free conversation,

00:34:21.420 --> 00:34:24.740
Dead Meat James podcast, Dead Meat podcast

00:34:25.990 --> 00:34:26.823
was a really fun discussion.

00:34:26.823 --> 00:34:29.240
Yeah, with James and Chelsea.

00:34:29.240 --> 00:34:33.110
And then similarly, spoiler-free
podcast conversation

00:34:33.110 --> 00:34:38.110
on Books On A Subway, like a
off the rails conversation.

00:34:39.500 --> 00:34:41.290
That one went places.

00:34:41.290 --> 00:34:42.150
So yeah.

00:34:42.150 --> 00:34:44.720
And then particularly for,

00:34:44.720 --> 00:34:47.150
You Made It Weird with Pete Holmes,

00:34:47.150 --> 00:34:51.030
two hours of talking about
a lot of topics that,

00:34:51.030 --> 00:34:54.530
and you know using the book
as a jumping off point,

00:34:54.530 --> 00:34:59.530
we talked about lots of
things in the realm of faith

00:34:59.700 --> 00:35:04.010
that I don't think we've ever talked about

00:35:04.010 --> 00:35:05.893
so if you're interested in that,

00:35:06.830 --> 00:35:10.430
definitely check out our
guest thing on Pete's podcast.

00:35:10.430 --> 00:35:12.370
But yeah, when it comes to podcasting

00:35:12.370 --> 00:35:16.260
and that pacing, podcasting gives me hope

00:35:17.520 --> 00:35:20.830
because it is that conversational,
drawn out, go in deep.

00:35:20.830 --> 00:35:23.230
I mean, what we're doing
right now is rewarding.

00:35:23.230 --> 00:35:24.730
It's a rewarding experience for us

00:35:24.730 --> 00:35:27.530
and I'm just glad that
people wanna listen,

00:35:27.530 --> 00:35:30.450
Well, I think there's also an authenticity

00:35:30.450 --> 00:35:32.517
to the platform, not just given the fact

00:35:32.517 --> 00:35:33.580
But you could've dressed

00:35:33.580 --> 00:35:35.380
a little more elevated, I'm just saying.

00:35:35.380 --> 00:35:36.213
Exactly.

00:35:36.213 --> 00:35:40.960
So it's like, why are we
worrying about this pretense?

00:35:43.330 --> 00:35:46.560
As you listen to us talk
about this, I'm sure,

00:35:46.560 --> 00:35:48.700
because you've chosen
to listen to a podcast,

00:35:48.700 --> 00:35:51.100
you've chosen to make guys

00:35:51.100 --> 00:35:54.110
who make primarily internet videos a part

00:35:54.110 --> 00:35:56.710
of your entertainment landscape.

00:35:56.710 --> 00:35:58.540
If you've made that decision,

00:35:58.540 --> 00:36:01.940
then you probably have chosen that

00:36:01.940 --> 00:36:04.170
because you have a distaste

00:36:04.170 --> 00:36:07.500
for the traditional way things are done.

00:36:07.500 --> 00:36:09.070
You're not watching the Today Show.

00:36:09.070 --> 00:36:11.390
You don't wanna see
these prepackaged clips

00:36:11.390 --> 00:36:14.849
that are moving at this ridiculous pace.

00:36:14.849 --> 00:36:18.670
You don't wanna think about, oh those guys

00:36:18.670 --> 00:36:21.240
are dressed like that, not
because it's who they are

00:36:21.240 --> 00:36:23.480
but it's who they think
someone else wants them to be.

00:36:23.480 --> 00:36:26.410
I understand that that
might be a huge turn off.

00:36:26.410 --> 00:36:28.180
We're just kind of being really honest

00:36:28.180 --> 00:36:30.000
about the way that we're talking about it

00:36:30.000 --> 00:36:34.340
because it is a game.

00:36:34.340 --> 00:36:36.640
When you go into someone else's territory

00:36:36.640 --> 00:36:38.810
and your, the whole point is to be there

00:36:38.810 --> 00:36:41.330
to appeal to their
audience so, very frankly,

00:36:41.330 --> 00:36:44.420
you can sell more books
because you believe

00:36:44.420 --> 00:36:46.400
in what you created and you wanna be able

00:36:46.400 --> 00:36:48.540
to write more and you want this thing

00:36:48.540 --> 00:36:50.860
to break outside of your fan base,

00:36:50.860 --> 00:36:53.030
you kinda have to swallow
some of those things.

00:36:53.030 --> 00:36:55.050
Well, you don't have to
but we have chosen to,

00:36:55.050 --> 00:36:58.923
without any shame, to
swallow some of those things.

00:37:00.086 --> 00:37:04.580
But I would say that it
is the more laid back,

00:37:04.580 --> 00:37:09.580
more authentic, new media
appearances that are more fun.

00:37:10.390 --> 00:37:15.390
But you know, the
traditional television media,

00:37:15.740 --> 00:37:18.230
you see how they're being
informed by the internet.

00:37:18.230 --> 00:37:21.070
It's like your point about the Today Show

00:37:21.070 --> 00:37:23.070
was you'd think that by the fourth hour

00:37:23.070 --> 00:37:24.400
that they could stretch things out

00:37:24.400 --> 00:37:26.780
and get a little more conversational.

00:37:26.780 --> 00:37:28.160
Obviously that's not the case

00:37:28.160 --> 00:37:31.670
because we're sitting there like, quickly,

00:37:31.670 --> 00:37:34.629
I mean our segment was so
short, even in the fourth hour.

00:37:34.629 --> 00:37:35.462
And we did so many things.
If you don't stop

00:37:35.462 --> 00:37:37.220
and zoom in on food,
somebody's gonna turn it

00:37:37.220 --> 00:37:38.520
to Good Morning America.

00:37:38.520 --> 00:37:39.390
Apparently.

00:37:39.390 --> 00:37:41.010
Right, is that the theory?

00:37:41.010 --> 00:37:44.030
Well, it really kinda mirrors

00:37:44.030 --> 00:37:46.973
what's happening online
in terms of click bait.

00:37:48.130 --> 00:37:50.690
Or I'll say the way that
even the Tonight Show

00:37:50.690 --> 00:37:53.650
and all the talk shows
have to create content

00:37:53.650 --> 00:37:55.803
that then works on the internet, you know?

00:37:56.960 --> 00:38:01.180
I mean, we found ourselves in situations

00:38:01.180 --> 00:38:03.330
that were not that different

00:38:03.330 --> 00:38:05.460
than Good Mythical Morning because of how

00:38:05.460 --> 00:38:06.730
the landscape is changing.

00:38:06.730 --> 00:38:11.370
Perfect example, the next
day we flew to Chicago

00:38:11.370 --> 00:38:13.080
and we were on the Windy City,

00:38:13.080 --> 00:38:14.490
I think is the name of
the show, it's like,

00:38:14.490 --> 00:38:16.200
I think it's the ABC affiliate.

00:38:16.200 --> 00:38:17.379
Yeah, the Chicago

00:38:17.379 --> 00:38:18.212
In Chicago.

00:38:18.212 --> 00:38:20.650
version of the Today Show, basically,

00:38:20.650 --> 00:38:21.863
or Good Morning America.

00:38:24.687 --> 00:38:25.520
And you know,

00:38:25.520 --> 00:38:26.550
I think that's actually airing today

00:38:26.550 --> 00:38:28.440
that we're recording this.

00:38:28.440 --> 00:38:31.040
We never sat down and had an interview.

00:38:31.040 --> 00:38:32.540
We said a couple things about the novel,

00:38:32.540 --> 00:38:34.193
they held it up, they plugged it,

00:38:36.090 --> 00:38:39.410
but we played this game.
What do they call it?

00:38:39.410 --> 00:38:41.540
I don't know but it was Feel
and Squeal, it was what we do

00:38:41.540 --> 00:38:43.130
Put your hand in a box

00:38:43.130 --> 00:38:45.773
and then you guess what
it is your feeling.

00:38:46.610 --> 00:38:50.088
Now the night before, on the Tonight Show,

00:38:50.088 --> 00:38:53.420
the guest before us was Reese Witherspoon

00:38:53.420 --> 00:38:57.280
and when she first came out,
she put her hand in a box

00:38:57.280 --> 00:38:59.200
and tried to figure out
what it is she was feeling.

00:38:59.200 --> 00:39:00.630
She played Feel and Squeal.

00:39:00.630 --> 00:39:02.670
Now I'm not saying that the Tonight Show

00:39:02.670 --> 00:39:07.170
and the Windy City have copied
us in something we invented.

00:39:07.170 --> 00:39:08.770
I don't even know if we invented it.

00:39:08.770 --> 00:39:10.020
I know that there was
I know another,

00:39:10.020 --> 00:39:11.560
it was an idea that was waiting to happen.

00:39:11.560 --> 00:39:14.450
Vanity Fair did something
called the Fear Box

00:39:14.450 --> 00:39:16.300
and it was very much the same thing

00:39:16.300 --> 00:39:18.610
we were doing with Feel and Squeal but,

00:39:18.610 --> 00:39:20.060
and who cares if it was before or after?

00:39:20.060 --> 00:39:22.090
It's an obvious idea
once you start thinking

00:39:22.090 --> 00:39:24.360
of let's have guests do things.

00:39:24.360 --> 00:39:26.298
But that's what everybody's doing now.

00:39:26.298 --> 00:39:29.448
It's like you find yourself
in Chicago on a morning show

00:39:29.448 --> 00:39:32.202
and they're playing the same thing

00:39:32.202 --> 00:39:34.500
that Fallon's playing, the
same thing that we're playing.

00:39:34.500 --> 00:39:36.180
And not to mention on the Today Show,

00:39:36.180 --> 00:39:37.760
the whole zooming in on something

00:39:37.760 --> 00:39:39.540
and guessing what it is,
well, we've done that as well.

00:39:39.540 --> 00:39:42.333
Again, this isn't a, hey we did it first.

00:39:43.459 --> 00:39:45.290
We're not saying we did it first

00:39:45.290 --> 00:39:47.880
but we make a show that is

00:39:48.790 --> 00:39:51.010
the pieces of the traditional shows

00:39:51.010 --> 00:39:54.540
that are designed to then be viral

00:39:54.540 --> 00:39:57.440
or to break out or to
make it seem more fun,

00:39:57.440 --> 00:40:00.350
well, we have a show
that is built primarily

00:40:00.350 --> 00:40:03.950
with those sensational things
in Good Mythical Morning.

00:40:03.950 --> 00:40:05.930
And so the conversation
that we started having,

00:40:05.930 --> 00:40:06.763
Yeah.

00:40:06.763 --> 00:40:10.330
was, and again, we talk
about this all the time.

00:40:10.330 --> 00:40:13.500
While we're traveling
and while we're supposed

00:40:13.500 --> 00:40:18.330
to be enjoying, hey, we
gotta book that's out

00:40:18.330 --> 00:40:19.517
and people are responding well to it

00:40:19.517 --> 00:40:21.270
and we get to go on the Today Show

00:40:21.270 --> 00:40:22.730
and we get to go on Fallon.

00:40:22.730 --> 00:40:25.750
We just continue, it's our
mode to just continue to work

00:40:25.750 --> 00:40:29.623
and to discuss and to figure
out, what are we gonna do next?

00:40:30.500 --> 00:40:33.730
I actually have more fun
doing that than not doing it

00:40:33.730 --> 00:40:37.150
so it's fine but what we kept saying was,

00:40:37.150 --> 00:40:38.430
hmm, you know we're constantly trying

00:40:38.430 --> 00:40:42.420
to innovate in lots of different ways

00:40:42.420 --> 00:40:44.680
but also within Good Mythical Morning,

00:40:44.680 --> 00:40:46.943
and it's just like when you go to a,

00:40:48.640 --> 00:40:52.720
for lack of a better word, a
local news show in Chicago,

00:40:52.720 --> 00:40:56.083
and they're doing the thing
that you do on your show,

00:40:57.408 --> 00:40:59.950
it's kinda like when
your mom got on Facebook

00:40:59.950 --> 00:41:03.550
and you were like, I don't think
Facebook is for me anymore.

00:41:03.550 --> 00:41:05.320
The thing that we started thinking is,

00:41:05.320 --> 00:41:07.670
all right, we gotta do something else.

00:41:07.670 --> 00:41:09.900
Now that they're doing it for the moms,

00:41:09.900 --> 00:41:12.737
now we gotta do something new that they,

00:41:12.737 --> 00:41:14.770
they can copy that if they want

00:41:14.770 --> 00:41:18.390
but I kinda feel like
the pressure to be like,

00:41:18.390 --> 00:41:21.870
all right, this has gotten
all the way to this point.

00:41:21.870 --> 00:41:26.040
Let's find something else.

00:41:26.040 --> 00:41:29.480
Which is a conversation
we're constantly having.

00:41:29.480 --> 00:41:31.870
We're constantly trying
to invent new things but

00:41:31.870 --> 00:41:34.970
Especially towards the end of a year

00:41:34.970 --> 00:41:37.530
when we're starting to think strategically

00:41:37.530 --> 00:41:40.230
about what 2020 looks like.

00:41:40.230 --> 00:41:43.361
We're just scratching the
surface of that conversation.

00:41:43.361 --> 00:41:44.194
Yep.

00:41:44.194 --> 00:41:46.040
But when it comes to
Good Mythical Morning,

00:41:46.040 --> 00:41:48.780
it's like as we talked about

00:41:48.780 --> 00:41:51.990
with Ear Biscuits, evolve or die.

00:41:51.990 --> 00:41:53.390
And we don't have the answers

00:41:53.390 --> 00:41:56.940
and so I actually, I'm at a
pretty unsettled point because,

00:41:56.940 --> 00:41:57.773
Unsettled.

00:41:57.773 --> 00:41:58.640
I think we've started,

00:41:58.640 --> 00:42:00.670
Even with a fresh pedicure,
you're still unsettled.

00:42:00.670 --> 00:42:01.930
Uh yeah, sadly.

00:42:01.930 --> 00:42:02.763
Interesting.

00:42:02.763 --> 00:42:07.170
Because I think we've
identified this challenge

00:42:07.170 --> 00:42:10.840
and we said, you know
you read the tea leaves

00:42:10.840 --> 00:42:14.070
in the Windy City show and
it's like, if they're doing

00:42:14.070 --> 00:42:17.750
what we're doing, then we need
to be doing something else.

00:42:17.750 --> 00:42:18.690
So it's like,
Hundred percent.

00:42:18.690 --> 00:42:21.000
So it's like not knowing what that is

00:42:21.000 --> 00:42:23.550
and absolutely knowing that it's not

00:42:24.430 --> 00:42:29.013
a big, drastic change that
happens overnight, you know,

00:42:31.050 --> 00:42:32.510
the way that everything's set up,

00:42:32.510 --> 00:42:35.820
the way that we produce
Good Mythical Morning,

00:42:35.820 --> 00:42:39.710
it's more like how do
we continue to evolve?

00:42:39.710 --> 00:42:43.310
Not how do we drastically
change what the show is.

00:42:43.310 --> 00:42:44.850
But even within that evolution,

00:42:44.850 --> 00:42:48.313
like knowing strategically
what's the long view.

00:42:53.470 --> 00:42:56.040
Identifying the problem but
then not yet having the answers

00:42:56.040 --> 00:43:00.820
to me is an unsettling
point and I think I feel

00:43:00.820 --> 00:43:05.820
a little bit more engaged in
the challenge of it right now

00:43:07.100 --> 00:43:10.610
than I have over the
past few years, I think,

00:43:10.610 --> 00:43:11.443
when it comes to Good Mythical Morning.

00:43:11.443 --> 00:43:12.810
Yeah, I agree with that.

00:43:12.810 --> 00:43:14.900
And it's by no means
not the only challenge

00:43:14.900 --> 00:43:16.120
that we're facing.

00:43:16.120 --> 00:43:18.228
I mean, creatively there's lot of things

00:43:18.228 --> 00:43:19.061
we're trying to figure out.

00:43:19.061 --> 00:43:20.090
I think it was interesting

00:43:20.090 --> 00:43:23.000
that we've been having this
conversation about innovation

00:43:24.845 --> 00:43:27.800
and then we go and basically

00:43:27.800 --> 00:43:31.023
every single appearance that we make.

00:43:32.510 --> 00:43:36.510
Also, let's talk about the fact that we,

00:43:36.510 --> 00:43:38.420
I don't know when those
videos are gonna come out,

00:43:38.420 --> 00:43:39.930
the ones that we shot for Vanity Fair, but

00:43:39.930 --> 00:43:41.737
Well, just talk about
'em, it doesn't matter.

00:43:41.737 --> 00:43:43.830
We did a lie detector thing, right?

00:43:43.830 --> 00:43:45.790
Well, we've already done
a lie detector thing.

00:43:45.790 --> 00:43:47.290
We've done a lie detector
thing on our our show

00:43:47.290 --> 00:43:48.123
and we've also done it

00:43:48.123 --> 00:43:49.220
With our moms but that's not
what you're talking about.

00:43:49.220 --> 00:43:52.710
But we've done a lie
detector thing for BuzzFeed,

00:43:52.710 --> 00:43:55.490
I think, like two years ago, right?

00:43:55.490 --> 00:43:57.700
And now we're doing
another lie detector thing

00:43:57.700 --> 00:43:59.480
with whatever outlet that was.

00:43:59.480 --> 00:44:00.710
Vanity Fair.

00:44:00.710 --> 00:44:04.140
And so I think it's a funny video

00:44:04.140 --> 00:44:08.870
but you start to get this sense

00:44:08.870 --> 00:44:12.810
that everyone is doing
the same, they're seeing,

00:44:12.810 --> 00:44:14.940
they're looking out,
they're seeing what works,

00:44:14.940 --> 00:44:16.220
and then they're trying to do it.

00:44:16.220 --> 00:44:19.400
Some people just do it exactly the way

00:44:19.400 --> 00:44:20.700
that they've seen it done.

00:44:22.310 --> 00:44:23.230
And it works.

00:44:23.230 --> 00:44:24.063
Yeah.

00:44:24.063 --> 00:44:27.240
Until it stops working
and so I think we have

00:44:27.240 --> 00:44:29.380
to be the ones to find the next things

00:44:29.380 --> 00:44:32.550
that we're gonna try that
other people will emulate.

00:44:32.550 --> 00:44:34.740
Well, and it's a tall order.

00:44:34.740 --> 00:44:36.810
We have an incredible team

00:44:38.214 --> 00:44:39.483
