00:47:08.267 --> 00:47:09.110
"with the timeline?"

00:47:09.110 --> 00:47:11.550
And somebody was like, "Shuffleboard."

00:47:11.550 --> 00:47:13.023
And so now we do that with shuffleboard.

00:47:13.023 --> 00:47:14.840
Yeah and then on our last tour,

00:47:14.840 --> 00:47:15.970
we started throwing axes.

00:47:15.970 --> 00:47:20.650
So I come back and I'm like
now, in the same genre,

00:47:20.650 --> 00:47:23.970
in the same, we also
call it a bucket of like

00:47:27.410 --> 00:47:30.580
guessing using some physical challenge

00:47:30.580 --> 00:47:31.820
and let's do that with axes.

00:47:31.820 --> 00:47:33.710
Now we haven't done that
yet but we're gonna do it.

00:47:33.710 --> 00:47:35.120
I'm just saying we're gonna do it

00:47:35.120 --> 00:47:36.830
so if somebody does it before us

00:47:36.830 --> 00:47:38.977
or has already done it,
I don't know about it

00:47:38.977 --> 00:47:40.320
and we are gonna do it.

00:47:40.320 --> 00:47:41.790
It's tough to keep up
with who's done what,

00:47:41.790 --> 00:47:43.060
that's for sure, and there's a lot of

00:47:43.060 --> 00:47:47.270
like convergent ideation on the internet,

00:47:47.270 --> 00:47:48.550
where you end up doing the same thing

00:47:48.550 --> 00:47:51.210
just because everyone is
thinking, everyone is,

00:47:51.210 --> 00:47:55.010
this is what I'm trying to
articulate in frenish is

00:47:55.010 --> 00:48:00.010
that everyone is ideating
in the same landscape,

00:48:00.080 --> 00:48:03.160
kind of in the same ways and I think that,

00:48:03.160 --> 00:48:05.390
and I don't know if we're
capable of it, let me just say,

00:48:05.390 --> 00:48:06.700
I don't know if we're capable of it

00:48:06.700 --> 00:48:10.140
and I don't know if you can do
it within GMM and not kill it

00:48:11.050 --> 00:48:13.700
but I think the challenge
that we're trying to approach

00:48:13.700 --> 00:48:18.700
is how do you level up
the genre as opposed to

00:48:18.970 --> 00:48:22.280
just reinventing the wheel

00:48:22.280 --> 00:48:25.140
and just making it a little
bit flashier of a wheel.

00:48:25.140 --> 00:48:26.920
Yeah, 'cause you look at
That's the conversation.

00:48:26.920 --> 00:48:29.250
'Cause you look at Fallon,
how long can Fallon

00:48:29.250 --> 00:48:32.320
just have top notch
celebrity guests come in

00:48:32.320 --> 00:48:34.470
and put their hands in
things or drink things,

00:48:34.470 --> 00:48:35.910
the same stuff that we're doing,

00:48:35.910 --> 00:48:38.100
he can probably do it a
lot longer than we have

00:48:38.100 --> 00:48:40.640
because it's a television institution.

00:48:40.640 --> 00:48:45.640
And as much as I'd like to
think and maybe for some people,

00:48:45.652 --> 00:48:48.490
GMM is starting to feel like
an internet institution,

00:48:48.490 --> 00:48:53.490
it's like we cannot,
we can't rest on that,

00:48:53.710 --> 00:48:54.690
whether it's true of false.

00:48:54.690 --> 00:48:56.930
I don't know, it doesn't
matter to me at this point

00:48:56.930 --> 00:49:01.631
and you know, what
matters is creating things

00:49:01.631 --> 00:49:05.580
that we're excited about
within the context of the show,

00:49:05.580 --> 00:49:06.760
if I'm just looking at the show,

00:49:06.760 --> 00:49:11.210
and that we also think will
work, and that is a challenge.

00:49:11.210 --> 00:49:16.210
I mean, on one stop on the
Bleak Creek Conversations,

00:49:17.700 --> 00:49:20.980
we would always answer
questions from the audience

00:49:20.980 --> 00:49:24.463
that were tweeted at us and one question,

00:49:25.670 --> 00:49:27.150
let's see, 'cause I'm trying to remember.

00:49:27.150 --> 00:49:28.560
I actually don't remember the question

00:49:28.560 --> 00:49:30.233
but the answer was basically,

00:49:31.960 --> 00:49:33.470
why do we want to write a novel?

00:49:33.470 --> 00:49:36.260
And we talked about our
passion for storytelling

00:49:36.260 --> 00:49:41.260
and once we had the
opportunity then our excitement

00:49:42.140 --> 00:49:45.567
to mine our shared
experiences and our friendship

00:49:45.567 --> 00:49:47.583
and like pour that into a novel.

00:49:48.670 --> 00:49:50.600
So much of it was about story though.

00:49:50.600 --> 00:49:53.140
But then the thing in my answer was

00:49:53.140 --> 00:49:54.913
there was another side benefit,

00:49:56.280 --> 00:49:57.830
something we didn't anticipate,

00:49:59.000 --> 00:50:02.980
with saying yes to the huge
project of writing a novel

00:50:02.980 --> 00:50:07.747
and that was it really
got us back in touch

00:50:08.730 --> 00:50:10.340
with the roots of our friendship.

00:50:10.340 --> 00:50:11.210
Um hm.

00:50:11.210 --> 00:50:13.640
So yeah, we created that documentary

00:50:13.640 --> 00:50:14.740
in order to make the connection

00:50:14.740 --> 00:50:17.250
between the novel and our
friendship in order to

00:50:17.250 --> 00:50:21.316
not just to sell the book but
honestly that's part of it.

00:50:21.316 --> 00:50:22.820
Yeah.

00:50:22.820 --> 00:50:26.560
But then it became such
a important project,

00:50:26.560 --> 00:50:30.150
just the documentary part, I
think it was so important to us

00:50:30.150 --> 00:50:31.840
to reconnect with our
friendship that we wanted

00:50:31.840 --> 00:50:33.780
to make sure that not only ticket holders

00:50:33.780 --> 00:50:37.290
to the Conversation events but
everyone who wanted to see it

00:50:37.290 --> 00:50:38.460
who's a fan of ours could see it

00:50:38.460 --> 00:50:39.630
and that's why we put it out

00:50:39.630 --> 00:50:41.160
as three episodes of
Good Mythical Morning.

00:50:41.160 --> 00:50:42.950
As we're recording this,
we're in the middle of

00:50:42.950 --> 00:50:45.880
the second day and we're
already seeing the response.

00:50:45.880 --> 00:50:48.010
I don't know how may views
it'll end up getting.

00:50:48.010 --> 00:50:50.790
You know, I think Part One
is doing doing pretty well.

00:50:50.790 --> 00:50:53.093
Here's the ironic thing, right?

00:50:54.060 --> 00:50:57.740
This documentary of us going
back to our childhood homes,

00:50:57.740 --> 00:51:01.623
and Part One is literally
visiting the homes we grew up in,

00:51:02.770 --> 00:51:07.000
is not taking off as fast as a video

00:51:07.000 --> 00:51:08.693
that's just about food, right?

00:51:09.690 --> 00:51:11.230
However, there are a lot of people

00:51:11.230 --> 00:51:13.690
who are voicing their opinions about it

00:51:13.690 --> 00:51:15.767
and saying, "I love seeing
this kind of content from you,

00:51:15.767 --> 00:51:18.200
"I love when you guys
get out into the world."

00:51:18.200 --> 00:51:23.120
And obviously there is no world

00:51:23.120 --> 00:51:26.010
in which we turn Good
Mythical Morning into that

00:51:26.010 --> 00:51:29.840
because I think even
if you've never edited

00:51:29.840 --> 00:51:32.040
a video in your life, you
probably have some appreciation

00:51:32.040 --> 00:51:34.100
for the fact that those documentary,

00:51:34.100 --> 00:51:37.010
the three part documentary
was a lot harder

00:51:37.010 --> 00:51:39.480
to put together than an episode
of Good Mythical Morning,

00:51:39.480 --> 00:51:41.790
like exponentially more
time went into that.

00:51:41.790 --> 00:51:44.035
Sure, so that can't become
Good Mythical Morning

00:51:44.035 --> 00:51:45.280
and that's not what's on the table.

00:51:45.280 --> 00:51:47.300
Seeing the people's response to it

00:51:48.320 --> 00:51:53.270
because it literally is taking,
putting into the GMM flow

00:51:53.270 --> 00:51:55.920
something that's completely unexpected

00:51:55.920 --> 00:52:00.920
and completely outside of the
genre that we've established.

00:52:01.660 --> 00:52:03.920
I'm not saying we're gonna
move in that direction,

00:52:03.920 --> 00:52:05.850
I'm just saying that it shakes things up

00:52:05.850 --> 00:52:08.800
and it gets our minds working

00:52:08.800 --> 00:52:09.633
Well, I think,

00:52:09.633 --> 00:52:10.790
in a different way.

00:52:10.790 --> 00:52:13.973
One thing that we know it already did was,

00:52:15.710 --> 00:52:17.010
I hope this happened.

00:52:17.010 --> 00:52:19.570
I think that it reminded people

00:52:19.570 --> 00:52:22.170
that the reason why Good
Mythical Morning works

00:52:22.170 --> 00:52:27.120
is because of the deeply
rooted friendship.

00:52:27.120 --> 00:52:29.670
It's not just about,
what are they gonna eat

00:52:29.670 --> 00:52:32.080
and gag into a trashcan, ya know?

00:52:32.080 --> 00:52:37.080
And I found myself saying
that in answering a question

00:52:37.620 --> 00:52:38.960
at the Conversations last week

00:52:38.960 --> 00:52:40.710
and then people started applauding.

00:52:42.948 --> 00:52:45.380
I mean, we didn't talk
about that with every stop

00:52:45.380 --> 00:52:46.213
but I think that it's,

00:52:46.213 --> 00:52:47.670
Well the people who've made a decision

00:52:47.670 --> 00:52:50.293
to come to a Bleak Creek Conversation,

00:52:51.190 --> 00:52:53.270
that's a very particular group of people.

00:52:53.270 --> 00:52:54.103
Sure.

00:52:54.103 --> 00:52:57.523
And those people are
like, "Guys, I'm on board,

00:52:58.667 --> 00:53:00.117
"not just to watch you eat,

00:53:00.117 --> 00:53:03.017
"I'm on board because of who
you are and your friendship."

00:53:04.180 --> 00:53:05.933
And so we get that.

00:53:06.810 --> 00:53:08.460
But it's an interesting dance, right?

00:53:08.460 --> 00:53:12.920
Because if you lean completely into that,

00:53:12.920 --> 00:53:14.780
we probably wouldn't.

00:53:14.780 --> 00:53:16.440
Here's the ironic thing.

00:53:16.440 --> 00:53:18.550
Again, now if I could
speak Spanish or French,

00:53:18.550 --> 00:53:21.920
I'd be better at saying this
but the ironic part of this

00:53:21.920 --> 00:53:26.600
is that because we've chosen
to create Good Mythical Morning

00:53:26.600 --> 00:53:30.700
in a certain way that is
designed to kind of go out there

00:53:30.700 --> 00:53:34.020
and take advantage of the
way people's online behavior

00:53:34.020 --> 00:53:35.860
on the platform of YouTube,

00:53:35.860 --> 00:53:37.750
the way the algorithm serves up videos

00:53:37.750 --> 00:53:38.960
and the way people click on things,

00:53:38.960 --> 00:53:41.560
the titles and the
thumbnails and all that stuff

00:53:41.560 --> 00:53:43.300
that goes right into
that point of decision

00:53:43.300 --> 00:53:44.760
of somebody clicking on the video.

00:53:44.760 --> 00:53:48.393
Because we've done that for
seven years, 16 seasons,

00:53:49.730 --> 00:53:53.450
we then have the
privilege to write a book,

00:53:53.450 --> 00:53:56.620
make a documentary, get
people to buy the book,

00:53:56.620 --> 00:53:58.040
get people to watch the documentary,

00:53:58.040 --> 00:54:00.780
put it on a channel where
you've built this momentum

00:54:00.780 --> 00:54:01.963
doing all these things.

00:54:02.960 --> 00:54:07.960
And finding the balance,
I feel like, is the real,

00:54:11.430 --> 00:54:12.370
is what we're trying.

00:54:12.370 --> 00:54:13.970
We're trying to find the balance,

00:54:13.970 --> 00:54:16.360
that's what we're trying to achieve.

00:54:16.360 --> 00:54:19.830
And just to clarify, is it a
balance between two things?

00:54:19.830 --> 00:54:21.660
What are those two things
that you're saying?

00:54:21.660 --> 00:54:24.150
I'm saying that you
can't just go completely

00:54:24.150 --> 00:54:26.170
on one side of the spectrum,
which would be like,

00:54:26.170 --> 00:54:27.430
Confessional.

00:54:27.430 --> 00:54:30.180
Well, I was gonna go to
the other side first.

00:54:30.180 --> 00:54:31.242
Okay.

00:54:31.242 --> 00:54:35.840
The I'm just trying to get
you to click on a video,

00:54:35.840 --> 00:54:39.560
that's all I want you to do
is to just click on a video

00:54:39.560 --> 00:54:41.190
and I don't care whether you

00:54:41.190 --> 00:54:43.020
are an existing fan or a new fan.

00:54:43.020 --> 00:54:45.230
I just want you to click on a video.

00:54:45.230 --> 00:54:46.550
And then you've got the
complete opposite end

00:54:46.550 --> 00:54:49.300
of the spectrum is like, I
don't care about the algorithm,

00:54:49.300 --> 00:54:50.640
I don't care about the machine,

00:54:50.640 --> 00:54:52.830
I don't care about the game.

00:54:52.830 --> 00:54:56.850
I just to want to be my
unfiltered, authentic self.

00:54:56.850 --> 00:54:58.710
And I don't care about schedule,

00:54:58.710 --> 00:55:01.080
I don't care about how often we upload,

00:55:01.080 --> 00:55:02.990
I don't care about it
being a reliable thing

00:55:02.990 --> 00:55:04.540
that can be there every single day.

00:55:04.540 --> 00:55:06.580
It's just this completely authentic,

00:55:06.580 --> 00:55:08.920
if I feel like it, I will make a video.

00:55:08.920 --> 00:55:11.840
That is a part of YouTube
and there are people

00:55:11.840 --> 00:55:15.464
who are successful in that place.

00:55:15.464 --> 00:55:18.100
I couldn't make a video
for the past month, guys,

00:55:18.100 --> 00:55:19.630
because I've been doing X, Y, and Z.

00:55:19.630 --> 00:55:20.463
We don't ever do that.

00:55:20.463 --> 00:55:23.080
We say, we probably
shouldn't have made videos

00:55:23.080 --> 00:55:25.300
for the past month because
we're doing X, Y, and Z

00:55:25.300 --> 00:55:28.810
but we still gave you 20 videos this month

00:55:28.810 --> 00:55:31.290
or 40 or whatever the number is.

00:55:31.290 --> 00:55:36.113
So yeah I don't know exact,

00:55:37.050 --> 00:55:38.690
to bring it full circle back

00:55:38.690 --> 00:55:41.170
to self-promotion, right?

00:55:41.170 --> 00:55:43.220
Which is where this conversation started.

00:55:45.060 --> 00:55:50.060
I do think that whatever
the answers end up being

00:55:50.370 --> 00:55:54.010
to these questions and
what is striking a balance

00:55:54.010 --> 00:55:55.160
and how does that work.

00:55:56.870 --> 00:56:01.190
I will say that I am personally
much more interested in

00:56:01.190 --> 00:56:06.190
and excited about that documentary
going out to our audience

00:56:07.600 --> 00:56:09.960
and them being able to make
the connection between,

00:56:09.960 --> 00:56:12.800
oh, this is the world you guys grew up in.

00:56:12.800 --> 00:56:14.707
Like you tweeted, "if you want to know

00:56:14.707 --> 00:56:15.830
"why we are who we are",

00:56:15.830 --> 00:56:16.793
then watch this.

00:56:18.020 --> 00:56:19.610
Making that connection between us

00:56:19.610 --> 00:56:22.340
and then the work that we put out, right?

00:56:22.340 --> 00:56:27.340
Bleak Creek is a incredibly,
deeply personal expression

00:56:29.270 --> 00:56:32.310
of our highest creative aspirations.

00:56:32.310 --> 00:56:34.910
Not only is it something
that we feel great about,

00:56:34.910 --> 00:56:39.120
that we crafted it, but we
feel great about the fact

00:56:39.120 --> 00:56:41.050
that it is so tied to who we are

00:56:41.050 --> 00:56:45.723
and I would much rather be
able to do that on our terms.

00:56:46.900 --> 00:56:48.440
Being able to take that documentary

00:56:48.440 --> 00:56:52.840
and put it in place of
a show where we usually

00:56:52.840 --> 00:56:54.980
just sit at a desk and do something,

00:56:54.980 --> 00:56:57.223
having the privilege
to be able to do that,

